WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.573
One play on Spotify has to reach recommendations, royalty payouts, analytics, and fraud at once, none of them slowing the others.

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:13.146
The substrate that pulls this off is Kafka.

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:18.370
Trace one play.

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:23.595
The client sends an event; the producer hashes the user ID, picks a partition of the plays topic, and appends it to that partition's log.

00:00:23.595 --> 00:00:28.820
Three brokers replicate it, so the event survives any one of them dying.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:34.044
Downstream, two consumers read that same log at their own offsets.

00:00:34.044 --> 00:00:38.061
Here is the key idea.

00:00:38.061 --> 00:00:42.078
Kafka is not a queue, it is a log.

00:00:42.078 --> 00:00:46.095
A queue deletes a message once it is read.

00:00:46.095 --> 00:00:50.112
A log keeps every event until retention expires.

00:00:50.112 --> 00:00:54.129
So many consumers read the exact same log independently, each at its own offset.

00:00:54.129 --> 00:00:58.146
Recommendations reads in real time, royalties reads nightly, and either can rewind and replay.

00:00:58.146 --> 00:01:03.487
One guarantee to remember.

00:01:03.487 --> 00:01:08.827
Delivery is tunable, but in practice almost everyone runs at-least-once: never drop an event, tolerate a rare duplicate, and make the consumer idempotent.

00:01:08.827 --> 00:01:14.168
Exactly-once exists, for when money is on the line.

00:01:14.168 --> 00:01:18.162
Where does Kafka sit?

00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:22.156
MapReduce and Spark process data that already exists, in batches.

00:01:22.156 --> 00:01:26.149
Kafka is unbounded; it moves events as they happen, in milliseconds.

00:01:26.149 --> 00:01:30.143
It is what gets the data there in the first place, and makes it replayable.

00:01:30.143 --> 00:01:38.804
At scale this is everywhere: LinkedIn pushes a trillion messages a day, Netflix four million a second.

00:01:38.804 --> 00:01:47.465
That closes the module: storage that survives failure, computation that scales, and Kafka moving events in real time, on the same primitives throughout.
